# How the list of things to remove was derived
# --------------------------------------------
# The bar for this task is not "no telemetry", it is ZERO outbound requests
# between install and the user's first navigation. So nothing here was decided by
# reading code. A stock APK was installed on a factory-fresh emulator whose
# virtual NIC was recording (`emulator -tcpdump`, a capture BELOW the guest that
# an app cannot proxy around or opt out of), the home screen was launched, and the
# VM idled with no navigation:
#
#   ./scripts/android-smoke.sh --emulator --first-run-capture --capture-seconds 90
#
# Unpatched result: 47 outbound events once the harness's OS-noise allowlist (DHCP,
# mDNS, NTP, the emulator's own DNS) is removed, corroborated by the kernel's
# per-uid byte counters for the app (rx +5,702,294 / tx +145,138). Eight
# hostnames. Each was then attributed by ABLATION, not by inspection, because two
# plausible attributions turned out to be wrong -- see "two wrong answers" below.
#
#
# 1. The stories carousel and the sponsored stories  (merino + MARS)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# PocketMiddleware reacts to AppLifecycleAction.StartAction by scheduling two
# WorkManager periodic jobs, and a periodic job runs its first pass immediately --
# which is why a feature whose configured frequency is "every 4 hours" is a
# FIRST-RUN leak. The two jobs fetch:
#   merino.services.mozilla.com/api/v1/curated-recommendations
#       (service/pocket ContentRecommendationEndpointRaw.kt:20)
#   the MARS ad server, for sponsored stories ("spocs")
#       (service/pocket MarsSpocsEndpointRaw.kt:27-28). Note the host in a capture
#       depends on the build type: MarsSpocsEndpointRaw.kt:157,164-169 picks
#       MARS_ENDPOINT_STAGING_BASE_URL when BuildConfig.DEBUG, so a debug build
#       hits ads.allizom.org and a release build hits ads.mozilla.org. Same code
#       path, two hostnames; do not read the absence of one as the absence of both.
#
# The obvious switch is Settings.showPocketRecommendationsFeature /
# showPocketSponsoredStories (Settings.kt:207-212,235-240). Both are declared with
# lazyFeatureFlagBooleanPreference, and FeatureFlagPreference.kt:115-120 returns a
# DummyProperty when the featureFlag argument is false -- a property that reads
# false and DISCARDS writes. So flipping their shared featureFlag,
# ContentRecommendationsFeatureHelper, does more than change a default: it makes
# the two prefs unwritable, which is the "removed, not switched off" shape this
# project asks for. It costs nothing in usability, because
# settings/HomeSettingsFragment.kt:111-121 already gates the VISIBILITY of both
# toggles on the same helper -- the switches disappear instead of becoming two
# dead switches a user can flip with no effect. (Verified in the built dex: the
# argument to both setVisible() calls comes straight from the now-constant helper.)
#
# That alone is not enough, and this is the trap. There is a second path into the
# same fetch:
#
#   SettingsBackedPocketSettings.showPocketRecommendationsFeature
#       = settings.showPocketRecommendationsFeature || settings.privateModeAndStoriesEntryPointEnabled
#
# privateModeAndStoriesEntryPointEnabled is a plain booleanPreference whose default
# comes from the private-mode-and-stories-entry-point FML feature: `default: false`,
# but `channel: developer -> enabled: true` and `channel: nightly -> enabled: true`
# (nimbus.fml.yaml:1194-1207). The debug variant maps to the FML channel
# "developer" (fenix/app/build.gradle:441-447), so on exactly the build the smoke
# harness can drive, that OR-arm is TRUE and the recommendations refresh would
# still have been scheduled with both prefs false. Hence SettingsBackedPocketSettings
# is overridden to false as well, and the two startPeriodic*Refresh() calls are
# deleted outright rather than left behind an `if`.
#
# Checked in the built APK afterwards, across all 36 classes*.dex: zero invokes of
# PocketStoriesService.startPeriodicContentRecommendationsRefresh anywhere, and
# exactly ONE remaining invoke of startPeriodicSponsoredContentsRefresh --
# HomeSettingsFragment.setupPreferences$lambda$6$0, the OnPreferenceChangeListener
# of the checkbox that the same commit makes invisible.
#
#
# 2. The two default top-site tiles fetch their favicons from Mozilla  (BrowserIcons)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the one nobody would have found by reading the task title, and it is the
# reason this patch reaches into android-components.
#
# BrowserIcons' first "preparer" injects an icon URL that the site never declared.
# Upstream picks one of two, on Settings.enableMerinoManifest (FML default true):
#   MerinoManifestIconPreparer - an 886 kB bundled snapshot of Mozilla's Merino
#     manifest (service/merino-manifest/src/main/assets/manifest/manifest.json)
#     whose icon URLs all live on prod-images.merino.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net;
#   TippyTopIconPreparer - browser/icons/src/main/assets/mozac.browser.icons/
#     icons-top200.json, 234 entries pointing at google.com, www.gstatic.com,
#     lh[3-6].googleusercontent.com, abs.twimg.com, cdn.shopify.com and friends.
#
# Fenix seeds two default top-site tiles on first run from
# fenix/app/src/main/res/raw/initial_shortcuts.json -- google.com and
# wikipedia.org (plus five JP-region entries). Both are in both lists. So merely
# DRAWING the first-run home screen fetches two favicons from Mozilla's CDN,
# before the user has typed anything.
#
# Removing both preparers is not a preference for one source over the other; it is
# the difference between a source and none. With request.resources empty, load()
# in BrowserIcons.kt returns null without consulting a single loader and
# DefaultIconGenerator draws a local letter tile. A site's real favicon still
# loads the moment the user visits it, from the site itself: the icons@mozac.org
# web extension reports the page's own <link rel="icon"> resources, and
# MemoryIconPreparer / DiskIconPreparer replay what has already been fetched.
# BrowserIcons.kt is the only place in the tree that constructs either preparer,
# so one hunk covers Fenix, Focus, the PWA shortcut manager and the samples.
#
#
# 3. Onboarding, and why a debug build cannot prove this half on its own
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# FeatureFlags.onboardingFeatureEnabled was `!Config.channel.isDebug`: the
# onboarding flow is ALREADY off on the debug APK the smoke harness can drive. A
# first-run capture on a debug build therefore says nothing whatever about
# onboarding -- the same trap that makes --check-aboutconfig refuse to run on a
# debuggable build (docs/android/SMOKE.md). Two consequences:
#
#   * the constant becomes false unconditionally, so release, beta and nightly get
#     the behaviour the debug build already had;
#   * Settings.shouldShowOnboarding() returns false rather than computing
#     `featureEnabled && !hasUserBeenOnboarded || enablePersistentOnboarding`. That
#     second arm is the point: enablePersistentOnboarding is a real
#     SharedPreferences boolean (pref_key_enable_persistent_onboarding, reachable
#     from Secret Settings) which forces the flow on EVERY HomeActivity creation
#     regardless of the feature flag. Leaving it would mean the onboarding flow
#     stayed reachable on a shipping build. It is also what makes this change
#     falsifiable at runtime, which is how it was verified -- see below.
#     Deleting that body also deletes the
#     FxNimbus.features.junoOnboarding.recordExposure() it performed, a Nimbus
#     exposure event recorded merely by ASKING whether to onboard.
#
# What the flow contains, from onboarding.fml.yaml's `cards` default, is exactly
# what the board's acceptance lists: a terms-of-service card, a default-browser
# card, an add-search-widget card, a sync-sign-in card (the Mozilla account
# pitch), a notification-permission card and a toolbar-placement card. All six are
# OnboardingFragment pages, so all six go with the flow. The default-browser
# SYSTEM prompt in particular is only ever raised from
# DefaultBrowserPromptManager.maybePromptToSetAsDefaultBrowser(), called from
# OnboardingFragment and nowhere else.
#
# ContinuousOnboardingFeature is the same product spread over days instead of
# cards: day 2 and day 3 request the default-browser role, day 7 shows a Firefox
# Sync sign-in card. It runs from HomeFragment and BrowserFragment on every
# resume; its own gate is the continuousOnboarding FML feature (default false,
# nightly true). shouldShowContinuousOnboarding() returns false.
#
#
# 4. Terms of Use, and the store-review prompt
# --------------------------------------------
# The Terms of Use bottom sheet is a separate surface from the onboarding ToS card
# (TermsOfUseManager, shown from HomeFragment.kt:878-881 and BrowserFragment). Its
# gate is the terms-of-use-prompt FML feature, `default: false` with no channel
# override, so it fires on nothing we ship today -- but it is a Nimbus flag, i.e.
# exactly the kind of switch this project does not want to depend on, and the
# content is Mozilla's legal text presented for acceptance in a browser that is
# not Mozilla's. DefaultTermsOfUsePromptRepository.canShowTermsOfUsePrompt()
# returns false, which covers both surfaces at once and leaves no unused member
# behind (hasExceededMaxDisplayCount() goes with it).
#
# ReviewPromptMiddleware asks the user to rate the app, either in the Play Store
# or in an in-app sheet. It is a promo; it needs >= 5 app opens or 4 of the last 7
# days so it is not a first-run leak; and its Play-Store arm reaches Google
# through PlayStoreReviewPromptController. handleReviewPromptCheck() now dispatches
# DoNotShowReviewPrompt and returns; see the corrected dex evidence below for what
# that does and does not prove. It also removes the JEXL trigger evaluation, a
# Nimbus event-store read that ran on every home-screen resume.
#
#
# The two wrong answers, recorded because both looked obvious
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# prod-images.merino.prod.webservices.mozgcp.net survived the change in section 1,
# and the name made it look like the stories-image CDN. Two ablations on the
# patched build, each seeding shared_prefs BEFORE the first launch so the profile
# is genuinely fresh, settled it (70 s windows, same pcap parser as the harness):
#
#   pref_key_enable_contile=false            ads.mozilla.org GONE,
#                                            prod-images STILL THERE  (46 events)
#     -> so prod-images is NOT the sponsored top-sites feed. That also confirms
#        ads.mozilla.org IS the sponsored-tile feed, i.e. LW-M4-11's, reached via
#        HomeActivity.kt:628-630 -> contileTopSitesUpdater -> macTopSitesProvider
#        (MozAdsClientProvider, pinned to MozAdsEnvironment.PROD, which is why it
#        has no staging twin the way the spocs endpoint does).
#   + pref_key_show_top_sites=false          prod-images GONE, nothing replaced
#                                            it  (37 events)
#   + pref_key_enable_merino_manifest=false  prod-images GONE and REPLACED by
#                                            google.com, www.google.com,
#                                            www.wikipedia.org, plus QUIC to
#                                            142.251.x  (55 events -- WORSE)
#     -> the tiles' favicons, and switching the manifest off is a regression, not
#        a fix. Hence section 2.
#
# Recorded for LW-M4-11, as its brief asks: there is NO request to
# contile.services.mozilla.com in 153. Contile has moved to the MARS/ads-client
# endpoint, so a capture grepped for "contile" finds nothing even on a stock
# build. Grep for ads.mozilla.org.
#
#
# Things that look like first-run network calls on a RELEASE build and are
# invisible on the debug build we measure -- do not read "zero" as covering these
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fenix/app/build.gradle:404-441 gives the debug buildType TELEMETRY=false,
# ADJUST_TOKEN=null, SENTRY_TOKEN=null and NIMBUS_ENDPOINT=null, and WALLPAPER_URL
# is "" for every build type in our tree because build.gradle:396-401 reads it
# from a ${rootDir}/.wallpaper_url file only Mozilla's CI has. So:
#
#   * Adjust never starts here, which is why no Adjust host appears in any capture
#     above. That is not evidence for LW-M4-02. Handing over one detail: with
#     onboarding gone, FenixApplication.setupPostMegazord()'s !userHasBeenOnboarded()
#     branch is the one taken on first run, and it calls
#     distributionIdManager.startAdjustIfSkippingConsentScreen(). A stock first run
#     took that same branch, so this patch does not create the path -- but it does
#     make it permanent rather than one-shot, because nothing calls
#     FenixOnboarding.finish() until BaseBrowserFragment sees the first
#     non-external navigation.
#   * the wallpaper metadata fetch and thumbnail download
#     (wallpapers/WallpaperMetadataFetcher.kt:27, WallpaperDownloader.kt:30, both
#     queued from FenixApplication) build their URL from an empty string and go
#     nowhere. A build that ever gained a .wallpaper_url would fetch on first run.
#     That is also why WallpaperOnboardingDialogFragment cannot appear:
#     SessionControlController.kt:285-300 only navigates to it once enough
#     wallpaper THUMBNAILS have downloaded. Left alone deliberately -- it is the
#     wallpapers feature rather than a promo, and neutering an already-unreachable
#     path is a change no measurement here could confirm.
#   * DefaultTopSitesBinding.kt:62-64 skips the default tiles entirely when
#     `isReleased && region == RegionState.Default`, so a release build in an
#     unknown region gets no tiles and no icon fetch at all. The debug build we
#     measure always adds them, which is the conservative direction.
#
# Deliberately NOT in this patch, with owners: Nimbus messaging surfaces (the
# fullscreen message and the homescreen message card, via HomeActivity's
# showFullscreenMessageIfNeeded) and microsurveys -> LW-M4-03; sponsored top sites
# -> LW-M4-11; Glean -> LW-M4-01; remote settings -> LW-M4-08.
#
# One of those is not merely left alone, it is BROUGHT FORWARD, and LW-M4-03 should
# know: HomeActivity.kt guards `showFullscreenMessageIfNeeded()` and the initial
# navigateToHome() with `if (!shouldShowOnboarding)`. On a stock RELEASE first run
# shouldShowOnboarding is true, so that branch is skipped and the Nimbus fullscreen
# message is not evaluated until a later launch. With shouldShowOnboarding()
# constant-false it is evaluated on the first launch instead. This patch therefore
# moves a Nimbus messaging read into first run rather than adding one -- the same
# shape as the Adjust note above. Measured, not assumed: with LW-M4-03's
# no-nimbus.patch applied the 90 s first-run capture below shows no host that is not
# also present without it, and none belonging to messaging.
#
# One product question this patch deliberately does not answer, for the
# maintainers: initial_shortcuts.json still seeds a **google.com** tile and, in
# the JP region, a blog.mozilla.org "Firefox guide" tile. Those are Mozilla's
# default placements. Removing them is a branding/search decision (LW-M4-06 /
# LW-M4-07), not a network one, and after section 2 they no longer cost a request.
#
#
# Ordering, and mutations outside the diff
# ----------------------------------------
# No ordering constraint: none of these eight files is touched by any other patch,
# so scripts/check-patch-order.py derives no new shared-file pair. Nothing happens
# outside this diff (landmine L4): no file is copied, removed or generated, and
# .experimenter.yaml is not regenerated because nimbus.fml.yaml is untouched.
#
# Two assets are now dead weight rather than dead code: the 886 kB Merino manifest
# and the 38 kB tippy-top list are still packaged, because nothing here changes
# Gradle's asset packaging. Dropping them is a packaging change and a separate,
# smaller job; leaving them costs bytes, not requests.
#
#
