#   ContentRecommendationsFeatureHelper.isContentRecommendationsFeatureEnabled and
#       isPocketSponsoredStoriesFeatureEnabled   7 code units each -- the Kotlin
#       null-check on the parameter, then `const/4 v0, 0; return v0`.
#   SettingsBackedPocketSettings.getShowPocketRecommendationsFeature and
#       getShowPocketSponsoredStories   2 code units each.
#   PocketStoriesService.startPeriodicContentRecommendationsRefresh   ZERO invokes
#       anywhere in the APK.
#   PocketStoriesService.startPeriodicSponsoredContentsRefresh   exactly ONE invoke,
#       in HomeSettingsFragment.setupPreferences$lambda$6$0 -- the change listener
#       of the checkbox whose setVisible() argument, in the same dex, is the return
#       value of the now-constant isPocketSponsoredStoriesFeatureEnabled.
#   AppAction$ReviewPromptAction$ShowPlayStorePrompt and $ShowCustomReviewPrompt
#       are NEVER DISPATCHED -- but they are not absent from the dex, and an earlier
#       draft of this header claimed they were. The claim was "ZERO sget-object
#       anywhere in the APK"; re-running that exact grep on the built APK finds
#       FOUR, all in classes31.dex, so the sentence was false and is corrected here
#       rather than quietly dropped. Every one of the four is a COMPARISON, and the
#       instruction immediately after each sget-object is the areEqual that consumes
#       it:
#         ReviewPromptMiddleware.invoke   2 -- the `when (action)` arms that map both
#             actions to Unit (Kotlin compiles an equality `when` over objects into
#             sget-object + Intrinsics.areEqual + if-nez)
#         ReviewPromptReducer.reduce      2 -- the reducer arms that would turn each
#             action into ReviewPromptState.Eligible if one were ever dispatched
#       Those four are the ONLY instructions in the whole APK that name either type:
#       every other reference to the fully-qualified
#       Lorg/mozilla/fenix/components/appstate/AppAction$ReviewPromptAction$Show...
#       is inside the two action classes' own definitions in classes24. (Grep for the
#       bare word instead and you get 37 more hits, all in ShowReviewPromptBinding and
#       the two R8 inner classes named after it -- they are its
#       own method name `tryShowPlayStorePrompt` and R8's lambda classes named after
#       it, not references to the action. A substring grep is the wrong instrument
#       here; the type descriptor is the right one.) So no code path can pass either
#       action to Store.dispatch: a class that never loads the object cannot dispatch
#       it, and the two that do load it only compare. The only Store.dispatch in
#       ReviewPromptMiddleware is in handleReviewPromptCheck (26 code units, verified
#       in the dex) and its argument is DoNotShowReviewPrompt. Hence
#       ShowReviewPromptBinding never observes
#       ReviewPromptState.Eligible and PlayStoreReviewPromptController is unreachable
#       -- which is what the removed sentence meant, stated so that the check that
#       falsified it now confirms it.
#   MerinoManifestIconPreparer and TippyTopIconPreparer   never constructed: across
#       all 36 dex files there is not one `new-instance` of either type and not one
#       invoke-direct of either type's <init>, so no instance of either can exist at
#       runtime. Both classes are still IN the APK -- removing the call site does not
#       remove the class -- and 23 instructions still name one of the two types, all
#       of them inside the preparers' own compiled unit: 7 in TippyTopIconPreparer, 7
#       in TippyTopIconPreparerKt (the file-facade class the Kotlin compiler emits for
#       that file's top-level declarations), 1 in MerinoManifestIconPreparer, and 8 in
#       the three $$ExternalSyntheticLambda holders R8 generated for the preparer's
#       own `by lazy`. Those lambda holders ARE new-instanced, from inside the
#       preparer, which is why "grep for new-instance" alone would have been the wrong
#       check: the question is whether anything constructs a PREPARER, and nothing
#       does. (Re-verified on the combined APK described above, not only on the
#       one this patch was measured against.)
